  "reasoning": "Third revision, responding to canon:exit-gate's second fail verdict (retry_count 2, 5 blockers, high confidence). The load-bearing new finding: artifact_name_for_triple(triple) ignores package_name entirely, so two different packages declaring the same triple produce identical artifactName values, and the existing unique_by(.artifactName) dedup in action.yml (added to avoid redundant downloads, per its own comment) silently collapses them to one entry -- defeating placement for the second package. Root cause traced to crates/callisto-graph/src/matrix.rs:38-40 (artifact_name_for_triple) and confirmed PlatformTarget already carries package_name (crates/callisto-model/src/matrix.rs:70, threaded through from the package's own identity, workspace-unique by construction per PackageId uniqueness) but never uses it in artifact_name. Fix scope now includes a real Rust change: artifact_name_for_triple is replaced with artifact_name_for_package_triple(package_name, triple), embedding the already-unique package_name -- mirroring napi-rs's own convention of naming published platform-stub packages <package-name>-<platform>-<arch> for exactly the same disambiguation reason. Deliberately not made config-overridable: package_name already flows from the package's own, already-configurable identity, and the artifact name itself is a CI-internal correlation id (never published, never user-facing) with no independent reason to need its own override surface -- adding one would duplicate existing configurability for no benefit, matching this project's stated preference for leaning on existing mechanisms over inventing bespoke config. AC-001/AC-002 are new, testing the fix directly at both the Rust JSON-output level and the Action's dedup-consuming level. The remaining four blockers were addressed precisely: guard placement is now explicit in AC-003/AC-004's text (loop lives inside the INPUT_PUBLISH guard, not merely after NATIVE_MATRIX computation, closing the gap where an